    "q-78341": {
      "dom_id": "step-q78341",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, then ${A}^{4}=$",
      "worked_solution": "Computing ${A}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${A}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $A$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${A}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =O\\cdot O \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
