    "q-130635": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130635",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "If $B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, then ${B}^{5}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we compute ${B}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${B}^{2}=B,$ so $B$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${B}^{5}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{5} & ={B}^{2}\\cdot {B}^{2}\\cdot B \\\\  & =B\\cdot B\\cdot B \\\\  & ={B}^{2}\\cdot B \\\\  & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & ={B}^{2} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{B}^{5}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
